% Ten CBP rulings left for transcription BY HAND, through the editor's
% Scenario Editor: open this file, then Scenario Editor → Add → "Write it in
% English…" → From a document: the document's name ("ruling NY N348681") and
% the address of its text ("sources/cbp/N348681.txt"), Fetch text, Generate.
% The language model writes the facts the ruling states about each good, each
% citing its passage, with the templates of the whole tariff (tariff.le); review
% them, Insert, save, and run the query "subheading". CBP's own codes are listed
% here so the result can be compared — they are never facts.
%
% The rulings (September 2026 selection; not used anywhere else):
%   N348681  6101.20  a men’s knitted jacket from Vietnam
%   N357052  6204.62  women’s trousers from Pakistan
%   N329004  6115.95  a sock from China
%   N342139  6116.92  gloves from Pakistan
%   N351059  3920.10  printed polyethylene film from France
%   N348766  3917.33  a polyvinyl chloride (PVC) hose from Korea
%   N345059  6110.12  women’s knit sweaters from China
%   N360742  6104.43  women’s dresses from China
%   N334819  6211.11  men’s swimwear from Indonesia
%   N359773  3918.90  polyurethane flooring from Thailand
%
% README.md ("Adding a ruling by hand") reports a run of this path with an
% open-weight model.

the target language is: prolog.
scenario facts require provenance.

the knowledge base by hand includes these resources:
    tariff.

